# LibreWolf no-glean.patch  (Android)
# Owner: LW-M4-01 ("Remove Glean telemetry from the Fenix layer")
#
#
# Scope, and why this is a removal rather than a flag
# ---------------------------------------------------
# Gecko's own telemetry is already gone: patches/disable-data-reporting-common.patch
# plus patches/android/disable-data-reporting-android.patch (LW-M1-02, which had to
# flip mobile/android/moz.configure's MOZ_SERVICES_HEALTHREPORT implication the
# opposite way round from browser/). What is left is Fenix's Kotlin-side Glean
# integration, which has its own upload path and sits entirely outside Gecko.
#
# The Glean *SDK* cannot be removed. `org.mozilla.telemetry:glean` is a binary Maven
# artefact (67.3.2, gradle/libs.versions.toml:88); 150 files under
# mobile/android/fenix/app/src/main plus 1,112 generated org.mozilla.fenix.GleanMetrics.*
# classes record into it, the glean-gradle-plugin generates those from metrics.yaml,
# and Fenix's own unit tests assert on Glean's testGetValue(). Deleting the dependency
# or stubbing the API means rewriting thousands of call sites every four weeks, and
# LW-M2-01 already established that --enable-geckoview-lite (which does drop Glean
# from GeckoView) breaks the APK. So `mozilla.telemetry.glean.**` stays in the dex:
# 684 classes, and the string "https://incoming.telemetry.mozilla.org"
# (Configuration.DEFAULT_TELEMETRY_ENDPOINT, one occurrence, in classes36.dex) with
# them -- unchanged by this patch, in both cases. 140 of the 150 main-source files
# still name Glean afterwards. That is said plainly rather than hidden, because
# anyone who greps the APK or the tree will find it and should know why.
#
# What this patch removes is every piece of machinery that turns those classes into
# network traffic, at source level, in the places this tree owns:
#
#   1. the only call to Glean.initialize()
#   2. the two services whose whole purpose is to submit pings
#   3. the in-tree HTTP transport that carries a ping
#   4. the exported activity that lets any app on the device trigger an upload
#   5. the debug UI that submits pings on demand -- which, once (1) is gone, would
#      also freeze the app; see "the hang this patch had to fix"
#
# Item 1 is the load-bearing one and it is not a flag. It is not
# `uploadEnabled = false`, not `setCollectionEnabled(false)`, not a pref: the code
# that starts Glean does not exist any more, and neither does the file it lived in.
#
#
# Why "never initialize" is sufficient on Android, and how that was checked
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# On Android the Glean Kotlin SDK is the only thing that ever gives glean-core an
# uploader. Two facts, both from this tree:
#
#   * toolkit/components/glean/src/init/mod.rs:67-87 -- the `target_os = "android"`
#     arm of fog_init(). Its own comment reads "This should only be called in test
#     scenarios. In normal use Glean should be initialized and controlled by the
#     Glean Kotlin SDK", and it passes `uploader = None`.
#   * third_party/rust/glean/src/lib.rs:101-108 -- with cfg.uploader None the Rust
#     wrapper falls back to net::HttpUploader, and
#     third_party/rust/glean/src/net/http_uploader.rs:17-21 shows that type is a stub
#     which logs "TODO bug 1675468" and returns http_status(200) without opening a
#     socket.
#
# So even the shutdown-time fog_init() that FOG::GetSingleton() performs when
# telemetry.fog.init_on_shutdown is true (toolkit/components/glean/xpcom/FOG.cpp:100-115)
# has no way to send anything. The real uploader is Kotlin:
# GleanInternalAPI.initialize() sets Glean.httpClient from Configuration.httpClient,
# glean-core hands ping tasks back through OnGleanEventsImpl.triggerUpload, that
# enqueues mozilla.telemetry.glean.scheduler.PingUploadWorker, and the worker calls
# BaseUploader.doUpload. With no initialize() the chain is never armed: no uploader,
# no MetricsPingScheduler, no GleanLifecycleObserver, no WorkManager job. Recording
# calls in the 150 files that remain go through glean-core's dispatcher, which queues
# pre-init tasks (third_party/rust/glean-core/src/dispatcher/mod.rs:141-143) and
# never flushes them, because flush_init() only runs from glean_initialize.
#
# That chain is not an argument from source reading alone. The pre-patch build's own
# logcat walks it, class by class, 63 times in a 14-minute run:
#
#   D FenixApplication: Initializing Glean (uploadEnabled=true})
#   I glean/Glean: Registering pings for org.mozilla.fenix.GleanMetrics.Pings
#   I glean/MetricsPingSched: The application just updated. Send metrics ping now.
#   D WM-WorkerWrapper: Starting work for mozilla.telemetry.glean.scheduler.PingUploadWorker
#   D glean/ConceptFetchHttpUploader: Submitting ping to:
#       https://incoming.telemetry.mozilla.org/submit/org-mozilla-fenix-debug/nimbus-targeting-context/1/...
#     at mozilla.components.service.glean.net.ConceptFetchHttpUploader.upload(ConceptFetchHttpUploader.kt:81)
#     at mozilla.telemetry.glean.net.BaseUploader.doUpload$glean_release(BaseUploader.kt:22)
#     at mozilla.telemetry.glean.scheduler.PingUploadWorker.doWork(PingUploadWorker.kt:157)
#
# Every named class is one this patch either deletes or unhooks.
#
#
# The hang this patch had to fix
# ------------------------------
# Removing the initialize() call is not free. glean-core exports four functions that
# take the synchronous path -- block_on_dispatcher() and then core::with_glean(),
# which is `global_glean().expect("Global Glean object not initialized")`
# (third_party/rust/glean-core/src/core/mod.rs:81-97). With Glean uninitialized,
# block_on_queue() posts its task to the *unbounded* queue
# (dispatcher/mod.rs:158-176) that the worker only drains after flush_init, so the
# calling thread blocks for ever. Two of the four are called from the Fenix debug
# drawer, on the UI thread:
#
#   FenixOverlay.kt:104-105                       Glean.getLogPings(), Glean.getDebugViewTag()
#   DefaultGleanDebugToolsStorage.getPingTypes()  Glean.getRegisteredPingNames()
#
# The debug drawer is not nightly-only: SecretSettingsFragment.kt:298 toggles it and
# Secret Settings is reachable on any channel by tapping the About-page logo. So
# deleting the initialize() call *without* deleting the Glean debug tools would ship
# an ANR on a screen users can reach. That is why this patch also removes the whole
# org.mozilla.fenix.debugsettings.gleandebugtools package, its debug-drawer route and
# action, its nav-graph fragment and action, its BrowserDirection value and the
# `about:glean` address-bar command. Those exist only to submit pings to Mozilla's
# DebugView; removing them is not collateral damage. The dead
# "Custom Glean server URL" field in Secret Settings and its two helper functions in
# ext/Configuration.kt go the same way -- a privacy build should not ship a settings
# row that claims to point telemetry somewhere when there is no telemetry to point.
#
#
# The exported activity
# ---------------------
# The Glean AAR ships its own AndroidManifest.xml declaring
#   <activity android:name="mozilla.telemetry.glean.debug.GleanDebugActivity"
#             android:exported="true" android:launchMode="singleInstance" />
# and the manifest merger folds it into ours. Verified with `aapt2 dump xmltree` on
# the pre-patch APK, where it appears in the merged manifest with exported=true --
# and confirmed by driving it: on the pre-patch build
#   am start -n org.mozilla.fenix.debug/mozilla.telemetry.glean.debug.GleanDebugActivity \
#            --es sendPing baseline --es debugViewTag lwm401
# returned "Starting: Intent {...}" and was followed by fresh DNS traffic for
# incoming.telemetry.mozilla.org. Any app on the device, or anyone with adb, could do
# that. This patch removes the activity from the merged manifest with
# tools:node="remove" -- the mechanism the file already uses for
# WorkManagerInitializer and EmojiCompatInitializer. The class is inside the binary
# AAR and stays in the dex; what goes away is the ability to launch it.
#
#
# The in-tree transport
# ---------------------
# Fenix's uploader was mozilla.components.service.glean.net.ConceptFetchHttpUploader,
# from the in-tree Android-Components module :components:service-glean. Dropping the
# single `implementation project(':components:service-glean')` line from
# mobile/android/fenix/app/build.gradle removes all six of its classes from the APK.
# The component's own sources are deliberately left alone: mobile/android/focus-android
# and three android-components samples also depend on it, none of them is built here
# (assets/mozconfig.android sets --enable-android-subproject=fenix), and breaking a
# subproject we never compile would be a trap for whoever compiles it next. Glean's
# own HttpURLConnectionUploader stays in the AAR, but it is only ever constructed by
# Configuration's default argument and nothing in the app constructs a Configuration
# any more.
#
#
# What this patch does NOT do, and who owns it
# --------------------------------------------
#   * mozilla.components.lib.crash.service.GleanCrashReporterService stays. It records
#     CrashMetrics and submits Pings.crash, both dead while Glean is uninitialized,
#     but the crash-reporter wiring in Analytics.kt belongs to LW-M4-04 ("Remove the
#     Socorro crash reporter and its upload path"). Two patches editing the same
#     constructor call would collide; this one stops at the Glean boundary.
#   * org.mozilla.fenix.components.metrics.ActivationPing is left in place and now has
#     no caller at all (its only one was GleanMetricsService.start()). It reaches for
#     a Google advertising id, so it belongs to LW-M4-05 ("Strip Play Integrity,
#     Firebase and push messaging"). It cannot fire: nothing constructs it.
#   * mozilla.appservices.errorsupport.RustComponentsErrorTelemetry.register() calls
#     Glean.registerPings and is the ONE remaining call site into the Glean API from
#     outside the SDK in the patched dex. It is NOT dead code, and an earlier draft of
#     this header said it was -- the patched build's own logcat corrected that:
#       I glean/Glean: Registering pings for org.mozilla.appservices.errorsupport.GleanMetrics.Pings
#     The chain is FenixApplication.setupMegazordInitial() ->
#     AppServicesInitializer.init() (components/support/appservices/.../AppServicesInitializer.kt:28)
#     -> RustComponentsInitializer.init()
#     (third_party/application-services/components/init_rust_components/.../RustComponentsInitializer.kt:17)
#     -> register(). It runs at every startup and it is harmless: register_ping_type()
#     in third_party/rust/glean-core/src/lib.rs:944-961 branches on
#     `was_initialize_called() && core::global_glean().is_some()` and, with Glean
#     uninitialized, pushes onto PRE_INIT_PING_REGISTRATION instead of registering
#     anything with a live Glean. The component's only other entry point,
#     submitErrorPing(), is reached from reportRustError() and ends in
#     Pings.rustComponentErrors.submit(), which is dispatched and therefore queued
#     pre-init for ever. Registering a ping type cannot upload anything, and this
#     patch deliberately does not touch third_party/application-services.
#   * OnboardingFragment.startGlean() keeps its now-misleading name. Renaming it means
#     touching the onboarding composables, and LW-M4-10 ("Remove the Fenix first-run
#     flow") owns that file's shape. Its body no longer mentions Glean.
#   * The Adjust SDK is LW-M4-02's; it is a separate attribution channel that does not
#     go through Glean.
#   * BuildConfig.TELEMETRY is *not* what this patch relies on, and anyone measuring
#     this work has to know why. It is false on the debug build type
#     (mobile/android/fenix/app/build.gradle:406), so on the debug APK the smoke
#     harness tests, MetricController.create() returns DebugMetricController and no
#     MetricsService ever starts -- GleanMetricsService.start() was already never
#     called there. Glean.initialize() was called anyway: FeatureFlags.kt:36 makes
#     onboardingFeatureEnabled false on debug, so FenixApplication.maybeInitializeGlean()
#     ran at every start with Settings.isTelemetryEnabled defaulting to true
#     (utils/Settings.kt:618-621). That is why the pre-patch capture below does reach
#     incoming.telemetry.mozilla.org, and why the measurement is meaningful on a debug
#     build at all.
#
#
# Track, ordering and rebase
# --------------------------
# All 39 files are byte-identical between firefox-153.0.4 (./version) and
# firefox-153.0esr (./version.android) -- checked by extracting exactly these paths
# from firefox-153.0esr.source.tar.xz and cmp'ing all 39. The same hunks apply to
# either tree.
#
# **This patch has a real ordering constraint and it is not optional.**
# patches/android/no-adjust.patch (LW-M4-02) touches five of the same files, and in
# two of them the hunks interleave:
#
#   mobile/android/fenix/app/src/main/java/org/mozilla/fenix/components/Analytics.kt
#   mobile/android/fenix/app/src/main/java/org/mozilla/fenix/onboarding/OnboardingFragment.kt
#
# no-adjust removes the AdjustMetricsService/InstallReferrerMetricsService imports and
# list entries that sit immediately around the Glean ones, and the
# rtamoAttributionHandler line that was this patch's trailing context in
# OnboardingFragment. Measured, not guessed: generated against the pristine tree, this
# patch's three Analytics.kt hunks and one OnboardingFragment.kt hunk are REJECTED
# after no-adjust, and fuzz does not rescue them because the missing lines are context
# on both sides. The hunks below are therefore in **post-no-adjust coordinates**, which
# matches the list order in assets/patches/android.txt (no-adjust is listed first).
# Applying this patch to a tree without no-adjust will reject those four hunks. The
# other three shared files (app/build.gradle, AndroidManifest.xml, FenixApplication.kt)
# are in disjoint regions and only shift by an offset.
#
# The whole android list, in list order, was applied to a freshly extracted tree with
# `./scripts/check-patchfail.sh --targets=android --use-desktop-tarball`: exit 0, all
# patches applied, no rejects. Applying this patch alone to a mini tree carrying
# no-adjust reproduced the intended result byte for byte, and GNU patch really deletes
# the 20 removed files rather than leaving them empty (checked with `[ -e ]`, because
# `patch -p1` is invoked without -E and only removes empty output files when it can
# read a deletion from the headers -- these hunks carry `deleted file mode` and
# `+++ /dev/null`, and it does).
#
# Rebase notes -- the fragile parts, in the order they will break:
#   * the four exact-text removals in FenixApplication.kt, OnboardingFragment.kt and
#     Analytics.kt are the only places Glean is started or its services registered. If
#     a rebase drops one, do NOT re-check with grep. Re-run the dexdump scan described
#     under Evidence: the question is whether any class outside
#     mozilla/telemetry/glean/** still invokes Glean.initialize.
#   * the interleaving with no-adjust above. If either patch is regenerated, both
#     need re-checking together.
#   * 30 imports had to go with the code that used them, and the compiler will NOT
#     catch a leftover one. Every KotlinCompile task in this tree sets
#     allWarningsAsErrors
#     (mobile/android/gradle/plugins/conventions/.../ProjectPlugin.kt:229-232), which
#     is easy to mistake for cover -- it is not: an intermediate build of this patch
#     compiled `fenix:assembleDebug` to BUILD SUCCESSFUL while carrying eight unused
#     imports, because kotlinc has no unused-import diagnostic at all. `mach gradle
#     fenix:ktlint` is what catches them. One import that LOOKS unused must stay:
#     androidx.compose.runtime.getValue in FenixOverlay.kt, the operator import that
#     `by remember` needs.
#   * if upstream adds a caller of Glean.getLogPings, getDebugViewTag,
#     getRegisteredPingNames or getDebugViewTag from product code, it will hang. Those
#     are the only synchronous glean-core entry points reachable from Kotlin without a
#     was_initialize_called() guard.
#
#
# Evidence
# --------
# Two APKs from the same tree, differing only by this patch, same fat GeckoView AAR
# and same MOZ_BUILD_DATE, both produced with `scripts/android-apk.sh --skip-gecko`
# (Gradle only -- LW-M5-02 established that Kotlin-layer work leaves libxul.so
# untouched).
#
